            <div xml:id="d322">

                <pb facs="0263" n="213" xml:id="pg213"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 861.77/244</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Consul General at
                        Yokohama</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Scidmore</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1917-12-19T18:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">December 19,
                                1917, 6 p.m.</hi></date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>For John F. Stevens from Daniel Willard:</p>
                <p>Your cable 17th. Think you have decided wisely that Emerson and party should
                    remain in Japan until situation clears. Arrangements being made for ample credit
                    in Japan through American Ambassador at Tokyo as you request. There seems good
                    reason to believe situation in Russia will improve and that Emerson in short
                    time will be given opportunity to render service originally intended. Keep us
                    fully advised.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
